<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="molybdenum cofactor biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Description>&lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; The transition element molybdenum (Mo) has been long known as an essential micronutrient across the kingdoms of plants, animals, fungi and bacteria. However, molybdate itself is catalytically inactive and, with the exception of bacterial nitrogenase, needs to be activated through complexation by a special cofactor. There are several molybdenum cofactors, including |FRAME: CPD-582| (MGD), |FRAME: CPD-23445|, and others |CITS: [1587808]|. The chemical nature and biosynthesis of molybdenum cofactors have been investigated in detail in bacteria |CITS: [7836363][8514783][8514782][1587808][14761975]| and plants |CITS: [16669776][16784786][12147719][16234918][9421926]|. All of the different cofactors are synthesized from |FRAME: CPD-4| (MPT). The variability of the molybdenum cofactors found in bacteria is achieved by the attachment of GMP, AMP, IMP, or CMP to the phosphate group of MPT, and by attachment of different ligands (different number of oxo and sulfo groups, or amino acids from the enzyme) to the metal. The MPT structure is conserved in all organisms and it has been demonstrated that its biosynthesis is preserved in bacteria and eukaryotes alike. It is produced from |FRAME: GTP| via |FRAME: PRECURSOR-Z| (precursor Z) in three steps catalyzed by |FRAME: EC-4.1.99.22|, |FRAME: EC-4.6.1.17|, and |FRAME: EC-2.8.1.12| |CITS: [7890743][23627491]|. &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; All the molybdenum cofactors are derived from |FRAME: CPD-4|, whose biosynthesis is described in the pathway |FRAME: PWY-6823|. The insertion of Mo into molybdopterin, forming the biologically active prosthetic group, is catalyzed in eukaryotes by a multifunctional two-domain protein. The protein first activates |FRAME: CPD-4| to |FRAME: CPD-8122| |CITS: [15504727][ 9341109][11554796][12590921][10823911]| and then inserts molybdate into the cofactor in a step that involves AMP cleavage |CITS: [16636046][15504727]|. In a somewhat analogous manner, in |FRAME:TAX-562| the MoeA protein mediates ligation of Mo to molybdopterin while the MogA protein enhances this process in an ATP-dependent manner |CITS: [17198377]|. The insertion of Mo coincides with the release of copper bound to the molybdopterin dithiolate sulfur |CITS: [15306815]| which seems to play a more stabilizing role of |FRAME: CPD-8122| than being actively involved in Mo insertion |CITS: [16636046]|. One difference between the prokaryotic and eukaryotic pathways is that some of the eukaryotic enzymes are fusion proteins with two functional domains, each catalyzing a different step in the pathway, while the bacterial proteins are single domain enzymes and catalyze only a single step.</Description>
